WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.910
- When you think of musical improvisation,

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:03.370
the first thing that comes to mind

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.080
might be jazz or the blues.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.560
But the practice of
improvisation is actually

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.367
a part of many musical
traditions all across the globe.

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:17.367
(drums slamming)
(gentle piano)

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.260
As a classically trained musician,

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.201
I've personally been working on developing

00:00:26.201 --> 00:00:29.040
my improvisational skills
over the past few years.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.600
As you can see from these charts

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.462
that detail how I spend my practice hours.

00:00:33.462 --> 00:00:38.059
(drums slamming)
(gentle piano)

00:00:38.059 --> 00:00:42.226
- All right, do it again,
do it again, do it again

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.720
- Today, classical musicians
are know for playing music

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.390
as written in the score note for note.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.960
But this wasn't always the case.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.450
This may surprise you,
but from the middle ages

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.080
to about the late 1800s or so,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.895
improvisation was actually a big part

00:00:58.895 --> 00:01:01.560
of music performance and training.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.590
So why has classical
musicians stopped learning

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:04.502
how to improvise?

00:01:04.502 --> 00:01:09.502
(drums slamming)
(gentle piano)

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.455
- In classical music,
improvisation dates back

00:01:11.455 --> 00:01:13.000
to the middle ages.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.590
Soloists would add
spontaneous embellishments

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.270
over familiar melodies.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:20.280
And the score provided
served as a skeleton

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.240
of the melody over which musicians

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.220
would add their own variations.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.450
An example of melodic
variation today might

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.770
be found at a sporting event
with the national anthem.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.090
Singers would typically add in variations

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.950
and fills while keeping melody

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.280
and lyrics still recognizable.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.050
Going overboard with
embellishment, however,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.450
is never ideal because it can easily lead

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.425
to a distasteful interpretation.

00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:45.638
(garbled singing)

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.920
(crowd laughs)

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.590
- Classical musicians also
improvise accompaniments

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:51.650
to melodies.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.730
In fact, the first record of improvisation

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:58.120
in Western music comes
from ninth century writings

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.391
that detail how to add counter melodies

00:02:00.391 --> 00:02:02.500
to Gregorian chants.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.910
Later, during the Baroque era,

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.760
this type of improvised accompaniment

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.330
became what is called basso continuo.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.761
They used a kind of music
notation called figured base

00:02:10.761 --> 00:02:13.920
which provides a baseline
along with symbols

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.884
that indicate what intervals to use,

00:02:15.884 --> 00:02:19.194
chord suggestions, and types of voicings.

00:02:19.194 --> 00:02:21.680
So a musician, say a keyboardist,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.780
would take this information and would

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.670
improvise with and over the baseline.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.180
Probably the most widely known example

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.840
of improvisation in classical
music is the cadenza.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.000
If you're playing a Mozart
concerto back in the day,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.220
towards the end there's
always a point where

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.950
the harmony reaches a
peak then everyone stops

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.160
and the pianist or
whatever instrumentalist

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.694
will do a solo, basically on
the themes from the main parts

00:02:52.694 --> 00:02:56.074
and traditionally that was all improvised.

00:02:56.074 --> 00:02:58.574
(piano music)

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.013
- Yeah

00:03:03.013 --> 00:03:03.940
- That was your theme.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.729
Maybe you would

00:03:05.729 --> 00:03:07.870
(piano playing)

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.669
So we're in G major.

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.336
(piano playing)

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:20.027
That's the orchestra,
the connector goes okay

00:03:20.869 --> 00:03:23.536
(piano playing)

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.180
- Okay, question, is this
improvised right here?

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.493
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, you just

00:03:41.493 --> 00:03:42.326
- Okay

00:03:42.326 --> 00:03:45.810
- Right and then maybe you'll
try to make it more fancy

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.385
- That was super well improvised.

00:03:47.385 --> 00:03:50.052
(piano playing)

00:03:52.963 --> 00:03:55.058
- I don't know, maybe you do

00:03:55.058 --> 00:03:57.725
(piano playing)

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.590
No, I don't know about that.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:08.630
- Yeah right, every
time you play something

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.669
really cool you just be like,
do, do, do, do, I don't know

00:04:11.669 --> 00:04:12.556
(laughing)

00:04:12.556 --> 00:04:13.620
You be killing.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.512
- No, no, no.

00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:17.300
A few soloists carry on the tradition of

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.320
improvising their own cadenzas,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.626
including pianist Gabriela
Montero and Robert Levin.

00:04:22.626 --> 00:04:26.010
Levin tells us, something
spontaneous sounds different

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.550
from something that is not,

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:29.882
and the audience benefits
from that in a performance.

00:04:29.882 --> 00:04:32.760
Whenever I've played improvised cadenzas,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.464
the audience gets very quiet.

00:04:34.464 --> 00:04:36.780
For the first time in most of their lives,

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.240
they're at a classical concert

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.970
where they don't know
what's going to happen next.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.590
I feel like as a classical musician

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:46.580
I should be able to say yeah,
I can improvise a cadenza.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.940
- You say it's a lost art because

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.260
they used to do it all the time.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.030
- They used to, but then now people

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.023
just play cadenzas that
are already written out.

00:04:56.023 --> 00:04:58.050
- I got the theme, y'all.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:58.883
- Okay.

00:04:58.883 --> 00:04:59.716
- Barney theme song.

00:04:59.716 --> 00:05:01.120
- Oh, geez.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.209
- The Barney theme song.

00:05:02.209 --> 00:05:04.900
(Twinkle Twinkle Little Star on piano)

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.363
- No, no, no, no.

00:05:06.363 --> 00:05:07.691
- Twinkle, twinkle.

00:05:07.691 --> 00:05:10.358
(piano playing)

00:05:17.853 --> 00:05:21.603
(Barney theme song on piano)

00:05:27.979 --> 00:05:28.979
- Oh, minor?

00:05:36.205 --> 00:05:39.955
(Barney theme song on piano)

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.261
- And then the orchestra might go

00:06:07.261 --> 00:06:11.511
(humming along with piano playing)

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.360
Blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.610
- Why do you think improvisation
disappeared from classical

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.410
when like they was the
nicest at it in the unit?

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.470
- Maybe this is partially the reason why.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.600
- Improvisation?

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.870
- Because all the composers wrote

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.110
their own versions of cadenzas.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:37.240
They would write it
out and I think back in

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.703
the day it was to facilitate and help

00:06:39.703 --> 00:06:44.507
performers that were
just lesser improvisers.

00:06:44.507 --> 00:06:46.130
- Guide them through.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:50.530
- Uh huh, they wanted
to cadenza to be good,

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:55.482
so they wrote one out or several out

00:06:55.482 --> 00:06:59.140
and then because they exist performers

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.800
would latch onto that and just learn that.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.760
Also during the 19th century
the industrial revolution

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.955
created the middle class
and now more people

00:07:06.955 --> 00:07:09.490
with disposable income were interested

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:12.040
in music performance and education.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:14.030
Rather than hiring professional musicians,

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.130
middle class households can now purchase

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:18.990
their own instruments
and perform themselves.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.580
Also the growth of
published sheet music gave

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.870
more people access to classical music.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.937
The priorities of a lot of classical music

00:07:26.937 --> 00:07:31.937
performances, recordings,
concerts, I think it relies

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.223
on a certain kind of rigid exactness.

00:07:38.223 --> 00:07:40.989
- Yeah, that's intimidating.

00:07:40.989 --> 00:07:43.950
- And I think it wasn't always like that.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.838
When you're playing a
piece of baroque music,

00:07:45.838 --> 00:07:50.838
even the trills which are
meant to be very spontaneous,

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:51.953
everyone plays it

00:07:51.953 --> 00:07:53.310
(piano trill)

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.923
like that so everyone will then

00:07:54.923 --> 00:07:56.060
(piano trill)

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.517
but whereas back in the
day they would maybe

00:07:58.517 --> 00:08:00.158
(improvised piano trill)

00:08:00.158 --> 00:08:01.160
or

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:02.450
(improvised piano trill)

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:03.444
or

00:08:03.444 --> 00:08:04.520
(improvised piano trill)

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.150
The 20th century also brought about

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.380
breakthroughs in recording technology.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.878
Now, musical performances
could be captured

00:08:10.878 --> 00:08:14.320
and replayed forever and this new reality

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.280
lead to the pursuit of perfection

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:17.681
which inevitably involves less

00:08:17.681 --> 00:08:20.700
spontaneity and less risk taking.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.930
Recordings of famous works by composers

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.170
such as Mozart and
Beethoven became canonized

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:30.228
as museum music with less demand
for improvised performances

00:08:30.228 --> 00:08:32.860
or as professor Robin Moore puts it,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.020
spontaneous innovations cannot occur in

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.610
music which is intended
to be more a replication

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:41.250
from 1790 than a musical event of today.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.620
Personally, I feel that
improvisation has been making

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.000
a sort of comeback into
the classical music world

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.020
thanks to many modern composers.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:51.330
And this has certainly been influencing me

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.610
as well as looking toward
other genres like jazz,

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:57.980
flamenco, and folk
music that still embrace

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.240
many forms of improvisation.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.930
I think improvisation is
a very valuable practice,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.863
very relevant to music
making, to listening,

00:09:06.863 --> 00:09:10.250
and to experience music
in a very spontaneous

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:11.430
and creative way.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:13.980
For me it's been very fulfilling

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.225
to learn how to improvise
in these different styles,

00:09:17.225 --> 00:09:19.600
try improvising with the band,

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.340
do improvisation experiments
with irregular pulses

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.220
and things like that.

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:26.736
So I really do hope to
see more of an emphasis

00:09:26.736 --> 00:09:30.060
put on improvisation as a practice

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:34.967
in both formal and informal
music education moving forward.

00:09:34.967 --> 00:09:37.384
(soft music)

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.829
If I were to give myself
advice I would say

00:09:46.829 --> 00:09:49.065
before you do anything else find friends

00:09:49.065 --> 00:09:52.870
to explore this with and friends that

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:55.419
can help guide you and jam with you,

00:09:55.419 --> 00:09:59.730
because I spent so much
time just on my own

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.880
at the piano trying to understand

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.309
certain concepts about jamming in general,

00:10:05.309 --> 00:10:09.440
but as soon as I started
to play with other

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.610
people our jam with
Adam being one of them,

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:16.800
it opened up a different perspective,

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.540
a different kind of
understanding about all of this.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.100
- I have my formula of improvisation.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:25.113
There is inspiration, there's imitation,

00:10:25.113 --> 00:10:26.880
and then there's recreation.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.450
The inspiration is what we're inspired by,

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:32.770
it's the template, it's
what catches our attention,

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.480
it's what gives us that creative feeling.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.270
My second step is imitation,
learning exactly what

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.070
that template is, learning how to play

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:41.250
that template backwards, forward,

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.830
learning it like the back of your hand.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.530
This is where the skill portion
comes in, the recreation.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.443
The most important step.

00:10:48.443 --> 00:10:51.195
Recreating, adding your flavor to it.

00:10:51.195 --> 00:10:54.950
That's like the formula that I use

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.553
for improvisation all the time.

00:10:56.553 --> 00:10:59.220
(playful music)

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:09.770
Yeah

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:12.025
- Thank you so much for watching

